.TH "SERIAL.REQUESTPORT" "3JS" "May 19, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Serial.requestPort \- Serial: requestPort() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Secure context:\f[R] This feature is available only in secure
contexts (HTTPS).
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]requestPort()\f[B]\f[R] method of the \f[CR]Serial\f[R]
interface presents the user with a dialog asking them to select a serial
device to connect to.
It returns a \f[CR]Promise\f[R] that resolves with an instance of
\f[CR]SerialPort\f[R] representing the device chosen by the user.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
requestPort()
requestPort(options)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]options\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
An object with the following properties:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]filters\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A list of objects containing vendor, product, or Bluetooth service class
IDs used to filter the specific device types made available for the user
to request a connection to.
If no filters are specified, the user is presented with a list of every
available device to choose from.
Filters can contain the following values:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]bluetoothServiceClassId\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A positive integer or string representing a Bluetooth service class ID.
This can be a 16\- or 32\-bit UUID alias, any valid UUID, or a valid
name from a \c
.UR https://github.com/WebBluetoothCG/registries/blob/master/gatt_assigned_services.txt
GATT assigned services key
.UE \c
\&.
.TP
\f[B]usbVendorId\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A positive integer that identifies a USB device vendor.
The \c
.UR https://www.usb.org/
USB Implementors Forum
.UE \c
\ assigns IDs to specific vendors.
.TP
\f[B]usbProductId\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A positive integer that identifies a USB device.
Each vendor assigns IDs to its products.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]allowedBluetoothServiceClassIds\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A list of positive integers and/or strings representing Bluetooth
service class IDs.
Bluetooth ports with custom service class IDs are excluded from the list
of ports presented to the user unless the service class ID is included
in this list.
This is true whether you filter the list or not.
.RE
.SS Return value
A \f[CR]Promise\f[R] that resolves with an instance of
\f[CR]SerialPort\f[R].
.SS Exceptions
.TP
\f[B]SecurityError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
The returned \f[CR]Promise\f[R] rejects with this error in either of the
following situations:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
A \f[CR]serial\f[R] Permissions Policy blocks the use of this feature.
.IP \(bu 2
A user permission prompt was denied.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]NotFoundError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
The returned \f[CR]Promise\f[R] rejects with this exception if the user
does not select a port when prompted.
.SH DESCRIPTION
When the user first visits a site it will not have permission to access
any serial devices.
A site must first call \f[CR]requestPort()\f[R] to prompt the user to
select which device the site should be allowed to control.
.PP
This method must be called via transient activation.
The user has to interact with the page or a UI element in order for this
feature to work.
.PP
Access to the site might also be blocked by the \f[CR]serial\f[R]
Permissions Policy.
If blocked, the user won\(cqt be prompted for access to devices.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Allow the user to select any device
This example prompts the user to select a device via
\f[CR]requestPort()\f[R] when a \f[CR]<button>\f[R] is pressed.
It does not include a filter, which means that the selection list will
include all available devices:
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]button\f[R] id=\(dqconnect\(dq>Connect</\f[B]button\f[R]>
.EE
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] connectBtn = document.getElementById(\(dqconnect\(dq);
connectBtn.addEventListener(\(dqclick\(dq, \f[B]async\f[R] () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  \f[B]try\f[R] {
    \f[B]const\f[R] port = \f[B]await\f[R] navigator.serial.requestPort();
    \f[I]// Connect to port or add it to the list of available ports\f[R]
  } \f[B]catch\f[R] (e) {
    \f[I]// The user didn\(aqt select a device\f[R]
  }
});
.EE
.SS Allow the user to select a specific vendor\(cqs device
In this case, a filter is passed to \f[CR]requestPort()\f[R] with a USB
vendor ID to limit the set of devices shown to the user to only USB
devices built by a particular manufacturer.
.IP
.EX
connectBtn.addEventListener(\(dqclick\(dq, \f[B]async\f[R] () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  \f[B]const\f[R] usbVendorId = 0xabcd;
  \f[B]try\f[R] {
    \f[B]const\f[R] port = \f[B]await\f[R] navigator.serial.requestPort({
      filters: [{ usbVendorId }],
    });
    \f[I]// Connect to port or add it to the list of available ports\f[R]
  } \f[B]catch\f[R] (e) {
    \f[I]// The user didn\(aqt select a device\f[R]
  }
});
.EE
.SS Allow the user to select custom RFCOMM\-based services
Although most devices expose SPP\-based communication through the
standardized Bluetooth Classic Serial Port Profile, some use custom
radio frequency communication (RFCOMM) based services.
These devices have a Service Class ID that is not in the standard
Bluetooth UUID range.
.PP
You need to pass the \f[CR]allowedBluetoothServiceClassIds\f[R] list to
\f[CR]requestPort()\f[R] to access these custom RFCOMM\-based services:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] myBluetoothServiceUuid = \(dq01234567\-89ab\-cdef\-0123\-456789abcdef\(dq;

\f[I]// Prompt user to select any serial port\f[R]
\f[I]// Access to the custom Bluetooth RFCOMM service above will be allowed\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] port = \f[B]await\f[R] navigator.serial.requestPort({
  allowedBluetoothServiceClassIds: [myBluetoothServiceUuid],
});
.EE
.PP
You can also use the \f[CR]bluetoothServiceClassId\f[R] filter key when
calling \f[CR]requestPort()\f[R] to prompt the user with a list of
filtered Bluetooth serial ports identified by Service Class IDs:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] myBluetoothServiceUuid = \(dq01234567\-89ab\-cdef\-0123\-456789abcdef\(dq;

\f[I]// Prompt the user to select Bluetooth serial ports with\f[R]
\f[I]// the custom Bluetooth RFCOMM service above.\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] port = \f[B]await\f[R] navigator.serial.requestPort({
  allowedBluetoothServiceClassIds: [myBluetoothServiceUuid],
  filters: [{ bluetoothServiceClassId: myBluetoothServiceUuid }],
});
.EE
